    <sys:String x:Key="S.Gpu.ReadOnly">This laptop has not shown it speaks the protocol Nextcalibur was built against, so nothing that writes to it is offered. Readings only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Gpu.NotFromHere">Not from here</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Gpu.ConfirmSwitch" xml:space="preserve">Switch to {0}?&#10;&#10;This changes which chip drives your screen, and takes effect at the next restart.&#10;&#10;Two things happen because of it, and Casper's own software warns about neither:&#10;    - Your Windows PIN will stop working and need setting up again.&#10;    - If BitLocker is on, the next boot can ask for your 48-digit recovery key. Without it the drive does not open. Find it first - it is in your Microsoft account at aka.ms/myrecoverykey, or wherever you saved it.&#10;&#10;Both happen because the change alters what the TPM measures when the machine starts.&#10;&#10;Go ahead?</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Gpu.RestartNow" xml:space="preserve">Restart now? Windows will first ask any application that is holding unsaved work; if you cancel there, nothing changes.&#10;&#10;No keeps the change waiting for whenever you next restart, as long as Nextcalibur is running.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S.Gpu.RestartRefused">Windows did not start the restart. Restart the machine yourself; the change is applied as the session ends.</sys:String>

